A||||7||Fformula88||Snow Tires||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||10-19-2001||10:55 AM||||Since its getting near that time of the year, I thought it would be nice to hear everyone's snow tire opinions!  I've been reading info on other boards and reviews, and there is definately a difference of opinion out there.  Id'd say there are three main tires everyone likes for one reason or the other... Bridgestone Blizzack's, Michelin Arctic Alpins/Pilot Alpins, and Nokkian Hakka's. <P>Everyone says the Blizzak's handle bad weather great/dry roads badly.  They wear quickly and only 55% of their tread is the special winter compound.<P>The Michelin's offer better dry weather performance (some claim nearly as good as all-season tires!), wear a little better than the Blizzaks but sacrafice a little bit of foul weather ability.  Their tread pattern doesn't look too good to me for snow removal, although its designed to be good.<P>The Nokkian's are really loved over the other two by those who drive them.  The Miata owners seem to really love these, and like Fiero's those aren't the easiest cars to drive in the winter.  They think they give Blizzak level bad weather performance without sacraficing the dry weather ability and lasting far longer than other winter tires.<P>Any opinions on these or others out there?  I want to get a set for my daily driver this winter since the 180 I did in traffic making a turn at 10 MPH last year convinced me that I may need them!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Here in Buffalo, they have to begood with ice, but moderate to deep snow traction is almost a must along with decent dry road ability since I commute on highways everyday which are probably dry more than snow covered with the tons of salt that gets poured on them!  Thanks everyone!<P>PS:  I know I can "search so don't even suggest it or you'll be cursed with a Buffalo snow storm this winter!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hartz||10-19-2001||11:06 AM||||I run Blizzaks on my Firehawk and love them.  They are the only way to keep this car moving in a straight line during the winter.  They make winter "tolerable."<P>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||14
Z||000002||Neal||10-19-2001||11:21 AM||||Ive run yokohama guardX on my car for 3 years running, the same set too.  I think this season will be their last as the tires do wear.<P>Ive also driven blizzaks, on a different car, and think the yokohamas are better, better both on snow and pavement.<P>When i need to replace the tires again it will be with the yokohamas.<BR>(disclaimer: it was a different car so it is possible the car made for these differences.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||10-19-2001||11:56 AM||||I had a pair of 14 inch tech wheels with dunlop DP-1's I think they were. They were pretty good in the snow and ice. I never got stuck in my fiero. But last year seemed to be a somewhat mild winter for me though. I also drove my snow tires all the way through spring. They wern't really that bad in the dry. I kind of beat the crap out of them though. I used them for 1 winter and through the spring. When I took them off the back ones had about 35-40% tread and the fronts were at about 80%  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<BR>I sold those with my 88 coupe. So I am snow wheel/tireless this winter. Be interesting to see how I'll do on 16 inch pep boys all seasons lol  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. I'll be mostly commuting on it 10-20 miles a day so I think ill be "ok".<P>Anyway, if the fiero I drive now ever broke down or got stuck somewhere I'd probably jsut take my plates off, take out my stuff and abandon it. haha||||reg||12
Z||000004||Fformula88||10-19-2001||03:07 PM||||I thought last winter was fairly bad overall as for commutting in snow around here.  Especially for driving.  Maybe I need to move up to Niagara country and get out of this treacherous snowbelt.<P>Last winter I tried to drive on the original 14 inch Firestone FR680's that came on my Probe to save my aluminum rims.  By mid-december I was freezing my rear off putting the aluminum rims with Perreli p4000's (not exactly snow tires) back on becasue the Firestones were SO bad.  <P>This winter it is certainly going to be different!!<P>Anyone ever ride on the Yoko's with crushed walnuts in them for added traction?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Areakon||10-20-2001||01:26 AM||||My recommendation would be the winter mark which is made by Kelly. We keep it as our in house snow tire. I've never had a complaint about them. I had them on my sunbird last year and I was going though 12" of snow with very little problem. My dad also runs them on the front of his shop car all year round. And he normally gets around 40,000 miles out of them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||85 gt fiero||10-20-2001||09:58 AM||||i keep mine inside this nice heated storage locker. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES AND SOON TO BE BRIGHT YELLOW||||reg||1
Z||000007||TRiAD||10-20-2001||07:51 PM||||My opinion...<P>Blizzaks are nice, but do wear quickly.<P>The Pirellis are AWESOME!<P>I'd never buy anything made by Kelly Tire. <BR>$20 tires don't make for high quality. My life depends on it. <P>Just my $.02<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
